  Worth knowing before you read it: what you asked for was a parameter, and the spec doesn't add one. [38;5;153mlogin()[39m's
  signature is unchanged. The reason is in the Problem section — its only caller has no [38;5;153muserId[39m to pass, and "persist
  across the app for other forms" describes a stored value with an owner, not an argument. If you'd rather have the
  literal parameter anyway, say so and I'll rework it; that's your call, not mine.

  The decisions most worth checking:

  - [1m[38;5;153mlogin()[39m keeps its signature[0m; the id goes [3mout[0m (log line plus [38;5;153muserId[39m on the returned object), and other forms get it
    by importing [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m rather than through login.
  - [1m[38;5;153mtracking.mjs[39m, not [38;5;153mtracking.js[0m — the [38;5;153m.mjs[39m extension is what lets Node unit tests import it without setting [38;5;153m"type": [39m
    [38;5;153m"module"[39m, which would break the CommonJS files in [38;5;153msrc/[39m.
  - [1m[38;5;153mindex.html[39m gains [38;5;153mtype="module"[0m, which means the page needs a local static server to view; ES modules don't load over
    [38;5;153mfile://[39m.
  - [1mThe id is forgeable by design.[0m It's client-generated telemetry. The spec calls out that any future access decision
    based on it is a security bug.
  - [1mFailed persistence warns rather than failing silently[0m, because a silent fallback would make every reload look like a
    new visitor.

  Please review it and tell me if you want changes before I move on to writing the implementation plan.

